{"name": "propose_test", "arguments": {"name": "Premium vs Environmental Messaging Test", "theories": ["T1", "T2"], "method": "Online survey with randomized messaging exposure", "sample": "500 Danish adults recruited via an online panel, balanced for age, gender, and geographic region. Participants are randomly assigned to one of two conditions: Condition A sees a product description emphasizing premium health benefits (e.g., \"state-of-the-art membrane for superior water quality and health benefits\"); Condition B sees the same description but framed as an environmental necessity (e.g., \"reduces plastic waste and delivers pure water for a healthier planet\"). Both conditions include",
"measure": "Willingness to pay (WTP) for a branded, differentiated water purifier (estimated price €300-€500). Participants also indicate likelihood to purchase (1-7 scale) and primary purchase driver (health premium, environmental necessity, or other).", "prediction_by_theory": {"T1": "Condition A will generate higher WTP and purchase intent than Condition B, because premium health messaging directly addresses the health premium driver.", "T2": "Condition B will generate higher WTP and purchase intent than Condition A, because environmental messaging directly addresses the environmental necessity driver."}, "decision_rule": "If the average WTP for Condition A exceeds Condition B by at least €50 (e.g., €400 vs €350), conclude T1 is supported. If Condition B exceeds Condition A by at least €50, conclude T2 is supported. If the difference is <",
"cost_eur": 18000, "weeks": 8}}
